From b75e5b3386c4387fe0cb3cba624ad3f1ef40075f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D. Zack Garza" <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:03:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/16] feat: run the image on hosts without a container
 runtime

Consumers on hosts without Docker, chroot or PRoot fell back to a distribution
Sage, and the import and version failures that follow were read as defects in
the libraries the image provides. The environment was therefore not
reproducible for them, and a real fault in the image was reported as one.

docker/sage-image.py fetches the published image from the registry without a
daemon, verifies the digest of every layer, unpacks it into a cache that
records the index, manifest, config and source revision, and runs commands in
that tree with bubblewrap's unprivileged user namespaces: no root, no chroot,
no ptrace. The current directory is mounted at /work, more paths with
--bind SRC:DEST, and `check` runs the environment check the image build runs.
A missing bubblewrap or a kernel without user namespaces is named before
anything runs.

docker/SAGE-ENVIRONMENT.md documents the supported invocations -- the image in
Docker or as a CI job container, and this script where neither is available --
that provisioning is owned by this repository rather than copied into
consumers, and that a distribution Sage is not this environment. AGENTS.md
points at it, and records that the image is published from develop.

Verified here with no Docker, no chroot and no PRoot in use: the script unpacks
the published image, runs its Sage (ZZ(2)**10, an exact determinant, the
partn_ref canonical label), mounts a consumer checkout at /work, and
reproduces the published image's bliss segfault. Not verified: a full `check`
(image's own Julia/Lean checks) and the same script on a host whose kernel
forbids user namespaces, which this host cannot simulate.
